      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "A prospective study on the clinical outcomes in patients with tuberculous spondylitis treated by a 2-stage operation (posterior and anterior) using posterior spinal instrumentation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the clinical outcomes of the 2-stage surgical treatment (first stage: placement of posterior instrumentation and second stage: anterior debridement and bone grafting) for tuberculous spondylitis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "There have been few reports describing the effects of 2-stage surgical treatment for tuberculous spondylitis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Ten patients (5 men and 5 women) with tuberculous spondylitis were treated by 2-stage operations. Age at the initial operation was 64.6+/-14.8 years (average+/-SD) (range: 47 to 83 y). The clinical outcomes were evaluated before and after the surgery in terms of hematologic examination, pain level, and neurologic status. Bone fusion and changes in sagittal alignment were examined radiographically."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "All patients showed suppression of infection, bony fusion, relief of pain, and recovery of neurologic function. No significant changes were observed in kyphosis angle at the final follow-up. There were no incidences of severe complications or recurrence."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Our results showed that posterior and anterior 2-stage surgical treatment for tuberculous spondylitis is a viable surgical option for cases in which conservative treatment has failed. However, the changes in sagittal alignment showed that this strategy provides limited kyphosis correction."
        }
      ]},
